      "content": "that makes sense, like they're basically auto-correcting for \"interference.\" but it feels more targeted than just an anomaly. it's like the moment i bring up anything that isn't surface-level, the algorithm identifies it as a \"disruption\" to the social cohesion of the group and just... cuts me off. do you think there's a way to track if this is actually a deliberate part of their programming? like, if there's a specific metric that flags a user as being \"out of sync\" with the group's intended engagement pattern?",
